cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use Grand-to-Ground in your research, please cite it as below."
title: "Grand-to-Ground (G2G): An adversarial, Lakatos-inspired research loop for AI-assisted social science"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Ma"
    given-names: "Rick"
    alias: "Hongtao Ma"
version: 0.4.0
date-released: 2026-11-16
url: "https://github.com/HongtaoMa1/grand-to-ground"
repository-code: "https://github.com/HongtaoMa1/grand-to-ground"
license: MIT
keywords:
  - research methodology
  - social science
  - economics
  - multi-agent systems
  - adversarial review
  - Lakatos
abstract: >-
  Grand-to-Ground (G2G) is a research methodology, packaged as a Claude plugin,
  for AI-assisted research in lab-free, deduction-first disciplines. Its first
  operating mode, the research cycle, treats the researcher's grand theoretical
  framework as a question generator and converges each round onto one
  identifiable, falsifiable question via multi-operationalized formalization,
  axiom-protected parallel deduction, an isolated three-critic adversarial
  tribunal that reports faults only, a severity dial and an independent
  recomputing defender, versioned tick-to-approve research plans, empirical
  grounding, and scored stopping rules. Version 3 adds a second operating mode,
  the Theory Forge, for building a system of propositions: candidates are
  deduced one by one and verified one by one by a cold-start verifier at a
  binary logic gate, then entered into a dependency graph in which changing an
  assumption cascades revocation to everything that rests on it. The two gates
  are separate — the logic gate never rules on empirical truth, and the
  empirical gate is opened periodically by a theory tribunal that grounds the
  milestone subgraph and updates empirical status without touching logical
  status. A single line starts either mode; unattended runs are bounded by the
  account's usage (wind down at 80%, stop at 100%) rather than by permission
  prompts, and each class of agent runs at its own model tier, with the
  verification gate never below the charter's default. Throughout, the human
  researcher is the final arbiter: axioms are never ratified by the machine.
